{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Signals · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Signals</h1>
    <p>
        A signal holds a value that the browser shows. When an action, a timer or another tab changes it,
        php-via sends the new value to the tabs that use it, and Datastar updates every element bound to it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="creating-signals">Creating a signal</h2>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$count = $c->signal(0, 'count'); // initial value and name
$step  = $c->signal(1, 'step');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>
        The first argument is the initial value, any JSON-serializable value: an integer, a string, a boolean,
        an array or null. The second is the name, which every signal needs: <code>$c-&gt;getSignal('count')</code>
        finds the signal by it, a template gets the signal under it, and the browser id is built from it. An
        empty name throws.
    </p>
    <p>
        Declaring a TAB signal again with a name the context already has returns the same signal and sets it to
        the new initial value. php-via logs a warning when that changes the live value.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reading-and-writing">Reading and writing</h2>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$count->int();      // as int
$count->string();   // as string; an array comes out as JSON
$count->float();    // as float
$count->bool();     // 'true', '1', 'yes' and 'on' are true; other values follow PHP's rules
$count->array();    // as array
$count->getValue(); // as stored

$count->setValue(42);
$count->increment();   // adds 1 and returns the new value; increment(-1) subtracts
$items->mutate(fn (array $list): array => [...$list, 'new item']);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>setValue()</code>, <code>increment()</code> and <code>mutate()</code> take no other arguments.
        With several workers, <code>increment()</code> and <code>mutate()</code> change a shared signal in one
        step, where <code>setValue($count-&gt;int() + 1)</code> can lose a write another worker made in between.
        <code>increment()</code> works on integers only.
    </p>

    <h2 id="syncing">When the browser gets the new value</h2>
    <p>Where the write happens decides how the value reaches the browser:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>In an action, the TAB signals it changed go to its tab when it returns, also when it throws, for
            the page and its components. The view does not render for that: call <code>$c-&gt;sync()</code> when
            the HTML has to change too.</li>
        <li>A write to a scoped signal broadcasts its scope, so every tab in it renders its view and gets the
            value. See <a href="#scopes">Sharing a signal</a>.</li>
        <li>Anywhere else, such as a timer or a <code>spawn()</code> task, call <code>$c-&gt;syncSignals()</code>
            to send the changed signals, or <code>$c-&gt;sync()</code> to render the view as well.</li>
    </ul>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->setInterval(function () use ($elapsed, $c): void {
    $elapsed->increment();
    $c->syncSignals(); // no action runs here, so send the value
}, 1000);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>$signal-&gt;markSynced()</code> drops a TAB signal's pending value, so neither the page nor the
        next sync sends it. Use it after a write the browser already shows.
    </p>

    <h2 id="binding-in-templates">Using a signal in the page</h2>
    <p>
        A signal's id in the browser includes the tab, so write it from the signal object, never by hand.
        <code>ref()</code> gives the reference for Datastar expressions, and <code>bind()</code> the
        <code>data-bind</code> attribute:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view(fn (): string => <<<HTML
    <div id="form">
        <span data-text="{$count->ref()}">{$count->int()}</span>
        <input {$step->bind()} type="number">
        <div data-show="{$visible->ref()}">...</div>
    </div>
    HTML);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>In a Twig template, each signal is a variable under its name, and the <code>bind()</code> function writes the attribute:</p>
    {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<span data-text="{{ count.ref }}">{{ count.int }}</span>
<input {{ bind(step) }} type="number">
<div data-show="{{ visible.ref }}">...</div>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        On a web component, bind a property instead: <code>$volume-&gt;bind('value')</code> and
        {% verbatim %}<code>{{ bind(volume, 'value') }}</code>{% endverbatim %} write
        <code>data-bind__prop.value</code>. See <a href="/docs/web-components#binding">Web components</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Datastar 1.0.4 updates a signal bound to a checkbox or a radio button on <code>input</code>, not on
        <code>change</code>. A script that changes such an input and dispatches <code>change</code> has to
        dispatch <code>input</code>, or bind with <code>__event.change</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="client-writes">Values the browser sends</h2>
    <p>
        An action's request carries the values the browser holds, and php-via stores them before the action
        runs. Which signals take them:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>A TAB signal takes the browser's value, so <code>data-bind</code> works on it.</li>
        <li>A signal with a scope belongs to the server: the browser cannot write it.</li>
        <li><code>clientWritable: true</code> or <code>false</code> sets the rule for one signal, in either direction.</li>
        <li><code>Config::withStrictTabSignals()</code> makes TAB signals the server's too, unless they are
            declared with <code>clientWritable: true</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$status = $c->signal('idle', 'status', clientWritable: false);        // only the server sets it
$note   = $c->signal('', 'note', 'room:lobby', clientWritable: true); // a shared field every tab can write
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A value the browser sends must have the type of the signal's initial value. A form that converts
        without loss is stored as that type, such as <code>'5'</code> from a text field for an integer or
        <code>'false'</code> from a radio group for a boolean. Any other value is refused like a write to a
        signal the browser may not write, and a TAB signal sends the server's value back. A signal declared
        with <code>null</code> takes any type. Dev mode logs a warning once per signal.
    </p>

    <h2 id="client-seeded">A value the browser holds first</h2>
    <p>
        Some values start in the browser, such as the section a page's own script reads from
        <code>location.hash</code>. Declare such a signal with <code>clientSeeded: true</code>. Its initial
        value is only the server's fallback: the page leaves the signal out, and every SSE connect gives it the
        browser's value before the view renders, until the server writes it. Declaring it again keeps the live
        value. It is a TAB signal, and the browser can write it.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$section = $c->signal('overview', 'section', clientSeeded: true);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The browser has to hold the value when the SSE connect of <code>via_head</code> runs, so declare it on
        <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> or in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> before <code>via_head</code>, in a layout of
        your own (see <a href="/docs/views#full-documents">Views that render the whole document</a>):
    </p>
    {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta data-signals="{ {{ section.id }}: location.hash.slice(1) || 'overview' }">
    {{ via_head() }}
</head>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Datastar applies attributes in document order, and a value declared in <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> misses
        the first connect, which then renders the fallback.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scopes">Sharing a signal</h2>
    <p>A signal without a scope is private to its tab. Pass a scope as the third argument to share it:</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Scope;

$count  = $c->signal(0, 'count');                       // this tab only
$likes  = $c->signal(0, 'likes', Scope::ROUTE);         // every tab on this route
$theme  = $c->signal('light', 'theme', Scope::SESSION); // every tab of this browser session
$online = $c->signal(0, 'online', 'room:lobby');        // the tabs in this custom scope
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A scoped signal joins its context to its scope, so its writes reach the tab. Its initial value counts
        only when the scope does not have the signal yet; every later declaration gets the live value.
        <code>Scope::ROUTE</code> and <code>Scope::SESSION</code> resolve to this route's and this session's
        scope. After <code>$c-&gt;scope()</code> set a shared primary scope, <code>signal()</code> without a scope
        throws a <code>LogicException</code>: pass the shared scope to share the signal, or
        <code>Scope::TAB</code> to keep it private.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-demo">
        <div class="docs-demo-header">A TAB counter beside a ROUTE counter</div>
        <div class="docs-demo-body">
            <p style="font-size: var(--font-size-0); color: var(--text-3); margin-block-end: var(--size-4);">
                Open another browser tab with this page open, then increment both counters. The TAB counter is yours alone; the ROUTE counter is shared.
            </p>
            <div class="scope-split" id="scope-demo">
                {{ tabDemo | raw }}
                {{ routeDemo | raw }}
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p>
        A write to a scoped signal broadcasts its scope, so every tab in it renders its view again. Declare it
        with <code>autoBroadcast: false</code> to send the value yourself: <code>$c-&gt;syncSignals()</code> sends
        it to the acting tab and <code>$app-&gt;broadcast()</code> to the scope. An action sends only its TAB
        signals by itself, so such a signal stays where it is until you do.
    </p>
    <p>
        Code outside a context, such as a server timer, finds a scoped signal by its name:
        <code>$app-&gt;getScopedSignalByName(Scope::routeScope('/'), 'likes')</code>. See
        <a href="/docs/scopes">Scopes</a> for who a scope reaches.
    </p>

    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/actions">Actions</a>: where most signal writes happen</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/views">Views</a>: when the HTML around the signals renders</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/scopes">Scopes</a>: who shares a signal</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/api#signal">API: Signal</a>: every method</li>
    </ul>
</article>

{% endblock %}
